38	\mathrm{toggle}\ob{}\bigtuple{y: \mathrm{Green}\ob{}}: \mathrm{Color}\ob{} = \mathrm{Red}\ob{}() \\[1pt]
39	\mathrm{toggle}\ob{}\bigtuple{y: \mathrm{Red}\ob{}}: \mathrm{Color}\ob{} = \mathrm{Green}\ob{}()
40	\end{array}
41	\]
42	The abstract function $\mathrm{toggle}$ that takes an argument of type $\mathrm{Color}\ob{}$ is considered to be implemented
43	by the two concrete definitions of $\mathrm{toggle}$ because they collectively accept all the types comprised by $\mathrm{Color}\ob{}$.
44	The rules for checking such covering are actually more general than that, and appear in Figure~\ref{fig:covered}.
45	The basic idea is that the domain type of an abstract function (the tuple of argument types, with any type parameters of the function existentially quantified~\cite{oopsla-2011-type-checking})
46	must be a subtype of the union of the domains of the concrete definitions.  The function $\noselfdom$ is used to compute these domain types;
47	the wrinkle is that the same rules are used to check methods as well as functions, and for methods, the type of the trait of object within which
48	the method is declared is \emph{not} considered for the purposes of this particular test.
49	
50	The rule \rncite{T-Program} furthermore ensure that the function declarations obey the No Duplicates Rule, the Meet Rule,
51	and the Return Type Rule~\cite{oopsla-2011-type-checking}:
